  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1151 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe focus of this forecast package remains on the potential for\nmultiple rounds of widespread showers with embedded thunderstorms\nand the most substantial rainfall this area has seen since June.\nThe storm system of interest is onshore tonight in the\nsouthwestern United States, with Hurricane Polo just off shore of\nthe Baja California. The moisture associated with Polo will flow\ninto the Southern Plains as the aforementioned storm system shifts\neastward toward the region. Forecast precipitable water values\nremain near record levels for this time of year, supporting\nefficient rainfall production. The heavy rainfall potential will\nmove from west to east across the area with time, beginning\nWednesday evening in areas west of Highway 75 in eastern Oklahoma\nand moving out by midday Friday - although chances for lighter\nrainfall will extend into the weekend. There has been a lot of\nbuzz about the overall rainfall totals, and confidence is highest\nin some of the higher end totals /2-4 inches, locally 6-7 inches/ \nbeing realized across portions of eastern Oklahoma and much less \nacross western Arkansas given recent model trends. The NBM QPF is \nhanging onto some of the higher amounts featured in older model \nruns, and some adjustments have been made to the NBM \ninitialization to show some lower amounts in the eastern part of \nthe area using varying amounts of the 12Z ECMWF. Some\nconsideration was given to issuing a Flood Watch on this shift,\nbut ultimately, there is enough uncertainty to warrant allowing\nlater shifts another look. One thing that will weigh heavily is\nthe impact of the persistent drought on how much rainfall the area\ncan take. The heaviest amounts are actually forecast across\nportions of the forecast area /south of I-40/ with the highest \nFFG, which is another reason for pushing the watch decision to \nanother cycle.\n\nFall-like temperatures will finally arrive for a multi-day period,\nbeginning with the rain impacts Thursday into Friday, continuing \nbehind a cold front that moves through Friday into Saturday and \nreinforced Sunday into Monday. This reinforcement should also lead\nto drier conditions into early next week. Of note, Tulsa has not\nseen a temperature below 60 degrees since May 25th and early next\nweek may break that streak.",
